          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The guidance I could retrieve gives two different office blood pressure thresholds, and they do not agree.\n\n**US cardiology guidance** (the \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\" summary) classifies adult office readings this way:\n- **Normal:** systolic below 120 and diastolic below 80 mm Hg.\n- **Elevated:** systolic 120 to 129, with diastolic below 80.\n- **Stage 1 hypertension:** systolic 130 to 139 *or* diastolic 80 to 89.\n- **Stage 2 hypertension:** systolic 140 or higher *or* diastolic 90 or higher.\n\nWhen the two numbers fall in different categories, the higher category applies.\n\n**European guidance**, as described in the same document, treats hypertension as starting at 140/90 mm Hg. It classes the 130 to 139 / 80 to 89 band as high-normal rather than stage 1 disease. The same reading is therefore labeled differently depending on which body's guidance is used, and far more people count as hypertensive under the US threshold. The \"Hypertension: when treatment starts\" summary notes that many people the US calls stage 1 are advised on lifestyle changes alone under European guidance.\n\n**Gaps in what I could retrieve:**\n- The retrieved material does not name the European document (ESC/ESH) or give NICE's (UK) thresholds. I can't confirm NICE's position from these sources, so it is not included above.\n- The passages do not explain how a diagnosis is confirmed, such as repeat readings or home or ambulatory monitoring.\n- The passages do not say how strong the evidence is behind either threshold. They describe what each body recommends, not how firm the underlying trial evidence is.\n\nIf you want, I can search again for the European and UK documents specifically. A clinician can also tell you which threshold applies in your country and what it means for you personally.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
